CORE broken-smt-backend no-new-smt wide-pointer-encoding
main.c
--wide-pointer-encoding
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ device read routed to MMIO handler: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
The wide pointer encoding recognises a constant integer address (here the
device register 0x10) as an integer address via is_integer_address(), so
the memory-mapped I/O instrumentation routes the read to __CPROVER_mm_io_r.
Full MMIO support also needs writes to device addresses not to alias real
objects, which requires a notion of valid integer-address regions; see
issue #6747's --mmio-region. That is why mm_io1 (which writes) remains
no-wide-pointer-encoding.
